WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.600
Since her film debut in 1994, Scarlett Johansson&nbsp;
has become one of the most recognizable and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.640
successful actors in Hollywood. Here are a few of&nbsp;
the most critically acclaimed roles that Johansson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.920
has portrayed throughout her career, from&nbsp;
independent efforts to blockbuster smash hits.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.960
Still Johansson's best-reviewed film on Rotten&nbsp;
Tomatoes, Lost in Translation was hailed as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:26.320
revelation upon its release in 2003, both for&nbsp;
its leading performance by Bill Murray and for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.440
Sofia Coppola's stylish and elegant direction.&nbsp;
However, it's Johansson's performance that gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.560
the movie the profound sense of existential&nbsp;
loneliness that Coppola's films are known for.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.640
Johansson plays Charlotte, a recent&nbsp;
college graduate visiting Japan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.840
Adrift in her life and uncertain about her&nbsp;
future, Charlotte befriends fading actor Bob,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.040
played by Murray, who feels similarly lost as he&nbsp;
flounders through middle age. The two keep each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.640
other company for a few days in Tokyo, forming a&nbsp;
deep bond over their mutual sense of alienation.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.840
The film landed Oscar nominations for Best&nbsp;
Picture, Best Director and Screenplay for Coppola,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.400
and Best Actor for Murray, although Johansson&nbsp;
was unfortunately snubbed. Regardless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.400
Lost in Translation was declared&nbsp;
an instant classic by many critics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.160
Terry Lawson of The Detroit Press&nbsp;
summed up the general appeal, writing,

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.480
"There is real magic afoot in Lost in&nbsp;
Translation — the sort that is created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.360
not at the wave of a wizard's wand, but by&nbsp;
the coming together of two wayward souls."

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.600
Although Johansson didn't get much screen time&nbsp;
in Avengers: Infinity War, her iconic portrayal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.040
of the MCU's Natasha Romanoff, otherwise known&nbsp;
as Black Widow, played a pivotal role in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:40.320
grand cinematic finale that was Avengers:&nbsp;
Endgame. As one of the few Avengers left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.520
standing after the Snap, Johansson plays&nbsp;
a hero worn down by loss and despair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:51.120
Endgame provides Black Widow with an emotional&nbsp;
sendoff, as she — spoiler alert — sacrifices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.000
herself to help save the world. However, the&nbsp;
cosmic dust had barely settled on Endgame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.560
before Marvel head honcho Kevin Feige announced&nbsp;
a Black Widow standalone movie in July 2019.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.280
It may have taken nine years since the first&nbsp;
Avengers movie for Johansson to get her own film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.520
but it's certainly better late than never&nbsp;
for her legions of fans. Although the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:17.040
was delayed multiple times, Black Widow is&nbsp;
currently slated to hit theaters in July 2021.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.000
While Johansson is primarily known for&nbsp;
her live action work, she has also lent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.040
her voice to a number of prominent films,&nbsp;
including The Jungle Book, Isle of Dogs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.400
and The SpongeBob SquarePants Movie. However, her&nbsp;
most acclaimed voiceover work to date was her role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.080
as an artificially intelligent operating system&nbsp;
in Her, written and directed by Spike Jonze.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.120
The near-future sci-fi film, which won an Oscar&nbsp;
for Jonze's original screenplay, chronicles the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:49.040
romantic misadventures of sad-sack writer Theodore&nbsp;
Twombly, played by Joaquin Phoenix, who finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.760
himself falling in love with the artificially&nbsp;
intelligent virtual assistant "Samantha."

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.640
It's easy to see why Theodore falls for Samantha,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.800
even though she has no physical form. Samantha's&nbsp;
soulful personality, humor, empathy, curiosity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.920
and intelligence can all be felt through&nbsp;
Johansson's voice alone. Samantha also conveys a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.600
certain wistfulness about the experience of living&nbsp;
that feels all too human, despite her artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:19.520
nature. Critics were charmed and dazzled by&nbsp;
Jonze's vision, with Empire magazine declaring,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:25.600
"Jonze has made a sweet, smart, silly, serious&nbsp;
film for our times, only set in the future."

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.360
Alongside Taika Waititi's Jojo Rabbit,&nbsp;
Johansson earned one of her first two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:38.080
Oscar nominations in 2019 for Noah Baumbach's&nbsp;
Marriage Story. A searing portrayal of divorce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.800
Marriage Story sees Johansson play Nicole,&nbsp;
a former teen star married to Charlie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.360
a theater director played by fellow Oscar&nbsp;
nominee Adam Driver. When Nicole wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.840
to move to Los Angeles to advance her career&nbsp;
even though Charlie wants to stay in New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.600
their already troubled union begins&nbsp;
to slide towards dissolution.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.800
Baumbach himself has said that&nbsp;
the film is semi-autobiographical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.160
partially based on his divorce&nbsp;
from actor Jennifer Jason Leigh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.040
While Baumbach's sympathies are clearly biased&nbsp;
in Charlie's favor, Johansson still brings an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.840
enormous amount of sympathy and relatability&nbsp;
to the character of Nicole. Critics gushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.640
about the film and the performances, with&nbsp;
Moira MacDonald of The Seattle Times raving,

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.360
"Johansson and Driver are remarkably,&nbsp;
heartbreakingly good in every scene."

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.720
Ghost World was an early critical success&nbsp;
for Johansson and an important transition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.720
from her career as a child actor. Though&nbsp;
she was only sixteen upon its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:36.880
the 2001 movie, co-written and directed by Terry&nbsp;
Zwigoff, is a mature and serious film about two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:41.760
young women facing an uncertain world after&nbsp;
their high school graduation. For Johansson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.560
Ghost World — adapted from Daniel Clowes'&nbsp;
comic book series Eightball — was also her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.760
first foray into a genre that would catapult her&nbsp;
into the highest ranks of international stardom.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:57.120
The movie stars Thora Birch as Enid, a&nbsp;
classic Generation X archetype: ironic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.800
lost, and darkly cynical. Johansson&nbsp;
plays the role of her best friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.360
Rebecca, who is slightly more tethered to reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.440
and their difference in outlook eventually&nbsp;
leads the girls to having a falling-out.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.120
"Some people are okay, but mostly I&nbsp;
just feel like poisoning everybody."

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.600
Ghost World was not very popular with audiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:17.840
but it was widely praised by critics, who&nbsp;
appreciated its offbeat sense of humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.880
emotional honesty, and unsparing depiction&nbsp;
of teenage angst. As Roger Ebert wrote,

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:27.360
"I wanted to hug this movie. It takes such&nbsp;
a risky journey and never steps wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.360
It creates specific, original,&nbsp;
believable, lovable characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.920
and meanders with them through their inconsolable&nbsp;
days, never losing its sense of humor."

00:05:36.876 --> 00:05:42.080
The culmination of Phase One of the MCU, The&nbsp;
Avengers proved that Marvel movies were here to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:47.600
stay. The movie grossed more than 1.5 billion&nbsp;
dollars and was beloved by both audiences and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:53.120
critics, with a certified fresh score of 91%&nbsp;
on the Tomatometer. With almost 25 minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.160
of screentime, Johansson's Black Widow is the&nbsp;
third most prominent character in The Avengers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:02.000
after Iron Man and Captain America.&nbsp;
Even if her role in The Avengers isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.320
as dramatically juicy as the one she gets&nbsp;
in Captain America: The Winter Soldier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.320
she still gets her fair share of heroic&nbsp;
moments, especially as the Avengers save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:15.360
New York from Thor's brother Loki and his&nbsp;
legions of alien henchmen in the final act.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.560
While critics lauded the movie's&nbsp;
action and special effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:22.960
many were equally impressed by the performances&nbsp;
of the Avengers themselves. In his review of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:27.520
the film for The Atlantic, Christopher Orr&nbsp;
wrote that Johansson's Black Widow is, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:31.520
"far more interesting than she was in&nbsp;
Iron Man 2," and that the cast in general

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:34.480
"[...] is excellent down the&nbsp;
line, and plays well together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.840
even — perhaps especially —&nbsp;
when their characters don't."

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:44.080
The 2014 Captain America sequel The Winter&nbsp;
Soldier was something of a welcome departure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:48.000
for the Marvel Cinematic Universe.&nbsp;
Phase 2 of the MCU had gotten off to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:54.160
a bit of a rocky start with the successful but&nbsp;
uninspired Iron Man 3 and the middling Thor:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:59.280
The Dark World. Both films had done well at the&nbsp;
box office, but it was clear the MCU needed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.560
fresh approach. The Winter Soldier delivered by&nbsp;
fusing its typical superhero fare with elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:10.080
of the spy thriller genre popularized by classic&nbsp;
films like The Parallax View and Three Days of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.000
the Condor. Critics were impressed with&nbsp;
The Winter Soldier's action set pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.880
and its topical narrative about the price of&nbsp;
freedom and "security" in the age of surveillance.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.520
As Black Widow is the primary Avenger featured&nbsp;
alongside Captain America in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.720
Johansson benefited from the film's acclaim, and&nbsp;
was subsequently prominently featured in Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:32.640
Age of Ultron. But while that film's&nbsp;
reputation may have faded over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.120
The Winter Soldier has emerged as an&nbsp;
MCU favorite, with Johansson providing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:41.920
a big part of its continuing appeal among&nbsp;
both casual viewers and Marvel fanatics.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:48.960
Johansson reunited with Iron Man 2 director&nbsp;
Jon Favreau for his 2014 passion project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:54.240
about a middle-aged chef and his bold attempt to&nbsp;
reignite his creative spirit. Favreau plays the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:59.040
titular chef, Carl Casper, and Johansson&nbsp;
is Molly, the hostess at the restaurant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.680
Casper worked at for 10 years before his&nbsp;
very public mid-life crisis meltdown.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:07.600
"You're not getting to me! You're not&nbsp;
getting to me! He's not getting to me!"

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.720
Favreau and Johansson have a&nbsp;
natural chemistry in this film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:16.080
stuffed with stars as it is with delectable&nbsp;
dishes, including Dustin Hoffman, Sofia Vergara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:21.760
and Robert Downey Jr. Johansson's Molly is just as&nbsp;
appealing as any of the cuisine on display, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:26.880
is no small feat in a movie guaranteed to have&nbsp;
you craving a gourmet meal from beginning to end.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:32.320
Critics were mostly charmed by the light comedy&nbsp;
— which sits at 87% on the Tomatometer — praising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:36.640
the cast, the soundtrack, and of&nbsp;
course, the delicious-looking food.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:39.760
Writing in Little White Lies,&nbsp;
Chris Blohm called the movie

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:43.600
"A sassy and mouth-watering&nbsp;
concoction, bursting with flavour."

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:50.160
Given her comic abilities and previous experience&nbsp;
as a sultry femme fatale-type in post-war Los&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.840
Angeles in The Black Dahlia, Johansson was a&nbsp;
no-brainer to join George Clooney, Channing Tatum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:00.560
Ralph Fiennes, Frances McDormand, and&nbsp;
Tilda Swinton in the all-star cast of Hail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:06.320
Caesar!, a Coen Brothers comedy set in 1950s&nbsp;
Hollywood. Johansson plays DeeAnna Moran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:11.200
a character based off of actress Esther Williams,&nbsp;
who performs in synchronized swimming routines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:15.760
that were popular at the time. When DeeAnna's&nbsp;
unplanned pregnancy threatens her career and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:21.280
the studio's bottom line, a studio fixer played&nbsp;
by Josh Brolin is tasked with covering it up.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:24.960
Although audiences had mixed&nbsp;
reactions, critics enjoyed the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:29.920
praising the Coen Brothers' playful spirit,&nbsp;
its performances, and its nostalgic reflection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:34.560
on Hollywood's golden age. The Guardian's&nbsp;
Mark Kermode wrote of the film as a whole,

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:39.680
"It's utterly chaotic and ridiculously indulgent,&nbsp;
and it would amount to intolerable cruelty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:44.720
were Hail, Caesar! not so consistently,&nbsp;
uproariously, affectionately funny."

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.760
Johansson has carved out a niche for herself&nbsp;
in the science fiction genre more than most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:54.960
her fellow A-list actors. In addition to&nbsp;
her many appearances in the MCU and Her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:00.160
the actor has also headlined Michael Bay's&nbsp;
The Island, the surprise global hit Lucy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:05.840
and the controversial live action remake of Ghost&nbsp;
in the Shell. But while Under the Skin is sci-fi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:10.400
this indie film has little else in common&nbsp;
with those big budget commercial affairs.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:15.360
A product of guerrilla filmmaking, Under the&nbsp;
Skin surrounded Johansson with real people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:19.600
not actors, and captured much of its&nbsp;
unscripted footage with hidden cameras.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:25.280
The movie is light on action in favor of a&nbsp;
detached, cerebral story about a feminine alien,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:29.680
played by Johansson, who uses her good&nbsp;
looks to hunt down men throughout Scotland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:34.800
and lure them back to her dark lair to consume&nbsp;
them. However, as soon as she appears to identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.760
more traditionally as a human woman, she&nbsp;
finds herself going from predator to prey.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:44.720
Although a box office failure, Under the Skin&nbsp;
found favor with critics and scholars who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:49.360
read it as a feminist allegory about gender&nbsp;
inequality. It also showed off Johansson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:54.720
continued desire to take on challenging and&nbsp;
cryptic roles outside of the usual blockbusters.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:11:01.600
Taika Waititi won the Best Adapted Screenplay&nbsp;
Oscar for his anti-hate satire Jojo Rabbit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:06.960
which made waves upon its release for its unique&nbsp;
take on World War II. The film stars Roman Griffin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:12.480
Davis as the titular Jojo, a young German boy&nbsp;
whose imaginary best friend is a caricature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:18.400
Adolf Hitler, played by Waititi himself. While the&nbsp;
film was marketed as a comedy, Johansson's role as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:23.680
Jojo's mother Rosie serves as the emotional core&nbsp;
of the film, as she struggles to balance being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:29.440
single mother to a blindly nationalist child with&nbsp;
hiding a Jewish teenager from the Nazi forces.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:33.360
Critics and audiences alike enjoyed&nbsp;
Jojo Rabbit, praising its humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:39.760
charm, and anti-hate message. However, as with&nbsp;
many comic takes on Hitler and Nazis, Jojo Rabbit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:44.240
generated its fair share of controversy,&nbsp;
with Joanne Laurier writing that the movie

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:49.680
"[...] inadvertently or not trivializes&nbsp;
Nazism and turns people away from a study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:53.680
of the concrete historical processes&nbsp;
that produced such horrific carnage."

